    ServiceNow Update Set record-definition for the `sys_dictionary` row that
    declares the `case` Reference field on the `x_casemgmt_case_task` custom
    table inside the scoped application `x_casemgmt`.

    Per AAP Section 0.5.7 (Data-Model Mapping verbatim) for x_casemgmt_case_task:

        Field         Type                          Constraints
        case          Reference -> x_casemgmt_case   Mandatory

    THIS FIELD IS THE PARENT LINKAGE that ties every case-task row back to
    its parent case. It is the single most important field on the entire
    case_task table - without it, a task is an orphan with no semantic
    connection to any case, and the "all tasks must be closed before
    resolving" gate (AAP Section 0.5.5) becomes inoperable. Every other
    field on x_casemgmt_case_task (subject, type, status, assigned_to,
    due_date) describes properties of the task; only `case` describes
    WHICH case the task belongs to.

    Critical behaviors that depend on this field (AAP Sections 0.4.1,
    0.5.5, 0.5.6, 0.5.7):

      1. STATE-MACHINE "ALL TASKS CLOSED" GATE FOR Resolved TRANSITION
         (../flows/sub_flows/validate_resolved_transition.xml,
          ../script_includes/x_casemgmt_CaseTransitionValidator.xml)
         Per AAP Section 0.5.5 verbatim:
            From         To        Required condition
            In Progress  Resolved  All linked x_casemgmt_case_task records
                                   have status = Closed
         The validator subflow and Script Include both perform the exact
         GlideRecord query specified in the agent prompt:
            new GlideRecord('x_casemgmt_case_task')
              .addQuery('case', current.sys_id)
              .addQuery('status', '!=', 'Closed')
              .query();
         If even one row is returned, the subflow throws a blocking
         form-level error with the literal text:
            "All tasks must be closed before resolving this case."
         (AAP Section 0.7.4 verbatim "Case-resolution-blocking rule
         wording"). The query above WILL NOT WORK without this dictionary
         entry; the field name `case` declared here is the column the
         GlideRecord query targets, and the reference target
         `x_casemgmt_case` is what makes `addQuery('case', current.sys_id)`
         resolve correctly when the parent case's sys_id is passed as
         the right-hand-side filter value.

      2. RELATED-LIST ELIGIBILITY ON THE CASE FORM - NECESSARY, NOT
         SUFFICIENT, AND CURRENTLY NOT SATISFIED
         (AAP Section 0.4.4 "Internal user UI" requirement: "configures
          Related Lists on the case form for both `case_task` (parent =
          current case) and `case_party` (parent = current case)")
         This entry's `<reference>x_casemgmt_case</reference>` element
         registers the inbound relationship that makes a tasks related
         list POSSIBLE on the case form. It does NOT create one. A related
         list renders only when the parent form's related-list
         configuration (`sys_ui_related_list` plus its
         `sys_ui_related_list_entry` rows) names it, and THAT
         CONFIGURATION WAS NEVER AUTHORED for this POC.
         Measured on the verification instance: `sys_ui_related_list` holds
         zero rows for `x_casemgmt_case` (and zero for any `x_casemgmt`
         table) against 1545 rows instance-wide, and on a real case record
         the form's `#related_lists_wrapper` renders at a bounding height
         of 0 CSS pixels with class `tabs_disabled` and no tabs - so a user
         cannot see a case's tasks from the case form even though the task
         rows exist. This is an open gap tracked in
         ../docs/PDI_LIMITATIONS_AND_KNOWN_ISSUES.md; keeping `<reference>`
         here is what allows it to be closed by authoring and capturing the
         related-list configuration.

      3. ACL "ASSIGNED ONLY" CASCADE FOR CASE_AGENT
         (../acl/x_casemgmt_case_task_*.xml)
         Per AAP Section 0.5.6: case_agent has "Assigned only" CRUD
         access. For tasks, "Assigned only" propagates THROUGH this
         field: a case_agent may read/write a task ONLY IF the parent
         case (resolved by `current.case`) satisfies the case-level
         "Assigned only" condition (assigned_agent = current user OR
         assigned_group contains current user). The ACL condition
         scripts on x_casemgmt_case_task therefore traverse this
         reference to evaluate authorization:
            current.case.assigned_agent == gs.getUserID()
              || gs.getUser().isMemberOf(current.case.assigned_group.toString())
         This dot-walk chain (`current.case.assigned_agent`,
         `current.case.assigned_group`) requires that this dictionary
         entry's `<reference>` correctly target `x_casemgmt_case` so the
         platform can dereference the parent case's columns at ACL
         evaluation time.

      4. SEED-DATA POPULATION VIA NUMBER LOOKUP
         (../seed-data/tasks/x_casemgmt_case_task_demo_*.xml,
          ../scripts/seed_demo_data.js)
         Per AAP Section 0.5.2 reference-resolution rules: "Case
         references (e.g., on case_task.case, case_party.case, demo
         task/party seed) -> look up x_casemgmt_case by `number`." The
         seed-data XMLs and the idempotent seed_demo_data.js script
         resolve the parent-case sys_id at seed time via:
            var caseGr = new GlideRecord('x_casemgmt_case');
            caseGr.addQuery('number', 'CASE0000001');
            caseGr.query();
            if (caseGr.next()) { task.case = caseGr.sys_id; }
         There are NO hard-coded sys_id literals in any seed file
         pointing into this column - all references resolve by the
         human-readable case number, per AAP Section 0.7.2 "no
         hardcoded sys_id constraint" applied to seed data.

      5. CASCADE-DELETE BEHAVIOR (reference_cascade_rule = cascade)
         (this dictionary entry's `<reference_cascade_rule>` element)
         When a parent x_casemgmt_case row is deleted, the platform
         deletes this row's child tasks with it.

         SUPERSEDES the earlier `none` value. QA finding F18 measured
         what `none` combined with `<mandatory>true</mandatory>`
         actually produces on this instance: deleting the parent case
         returned HTTP 204 and the child task SURVIVED, still holding
         the deleted parent's sys_id. Read back with
         sysparm_display_value=true the column answered
         {'display_value': '', 'link': '.../x_casemgmt_case/<gone>'} - a
         MANDATORY REFERENCE RESOLVING TO NOTHING. Three consequences,
         all measured or directly entailed:
           * The row can never be saved from its own form again. The
             mandatory field is unfillable from a value that displays as
             blank, so the record is permanently frozen.
           * Every surface that reaches a task THROUGH this column is
             silently distorted: the case form's Tasks related list, the
             Agent Workspace "My overdue tasks" report, and the
             "all tasks closed" gate in
             ../script_includes/x_casemgmt_CaseTransitionValidator.xml,
             which counts children by `case`.
           * "Manual cleanup surfaces operator awareness" - the original
             rationale - is not what happens. Nothing surfaces: the
             parent delete succeeds with no warning, no message and no
             audit signal naming the children it stranded, so the
             orphans are discovered only when a report goes wrong.

         WHY `cascade` AND NOT ONE OF THE OTHER FOUR OPTIONS. The
         platform offers none | clear | cascade | delete |
         delete_no_workflow | restrict:
           * `clear` sets this column to empty, which leaves the child
             holding an EMPTY mandatory field - still unsavable from its
             own form, and still absent from every related list. It
             trades a dangling reference for a missing one.
           * `restrict` refuses the parent delete while children exist.
             That would REVOKE A GRANTED PRIVILEGE: AAP Section 0.5.6
             gives x_casemgmt_case_manager Delete with no qualifier at
             all, and a manager whose delete is refused because a child
             task exists no longer has the access the matrix awards.
           * `delete_no_workflow` suppresses the children's own business
             rules and audit on removal - the opposite of the visibility
             the original rationale was reaching for.
           * `delete` removes the child but does not continue down any
             further cascade chain. It is equivalent here, because
             x_casemgmt_case_task has no children of its own; `cascade`
             is chosen for being the platform's canonical expression of
             a parent-child composition and for remaining correct if a
             grandchild table is ever added.
         `cascade` is therefore the only option that both keeps the
         manager's unqualified Delete working and leaves no row behind
         in an unrepresentable state.

         MEASURED after the change: a fixture case with one child task
         and one child party was deleted (HTTP 204); both children were
         then absent (HTTP 404 on direct read), and an orphan sweep over
         the whole of x_casemgmt_case_task and x_casemgmt_case_party
         returned zero rows whose `case` fails to resolve. A case with no
         children still deletes cleanly, so the Delete grant is intact.

         Note the sibling reference fields
         x_casemgmt_case.assigned_group and x_casemgmt_case.assigned_agent
         correctly keep an empty `<reference_cascade_rule/>`: those are
         ASSOCIATIONS to global identity records, not compositions -
         deleting a group must not delete the cases assigned to it. The
         distinction is exactly why this field, which is a composition,
         needs an explicit rule while those do not.

